<!-- THE PROBLEM -->
<section class="cx-problem cx-section" id="the-problem">
  <div class="cx-container">
    <div class="r">
      <div class="cx-eyebrow">The Industry Problem</div>
      <h2 class="cx-h2">Most contractors are broken.<br>Here is why project managers are done guessing.</h2>
      <p class="cx-sub" style="max-width:600px">If you have hired commercial contractors in the GTA before, you have likely experienced at least one of these. They are industry-wide failures — and they are the reason VNG was built.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cx-problem-grid stagger">
      <div class="cx-problem-card r">
        <h3>The Disappearing Act</h3>
        <p>You get a solid pitch, a competitive quote, and a promised start date. Then radio silence. Calls go unanswered. The team is tied up on another job. You are not the priority.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-problem-card r">
        <h3>The Change Order Ambush</h3>
        <p>The quote looked good. Then the change orders started. Suddenly you are 30% over budget on "unforeseen conditions" that any experienced contractor would have flagged at quote time.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-problem-card r">
        <h3>The Domino Delay</h3>
        <p>One trade falls behind. Nobody tells the next trade. They show up, find an unfinished space, and demobilize. What started as a three-day slip becomes a three-week delay. Your tenant is furious.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-problem-card r">
        <h3>The Finger-Pointing Loop</h3>
        <p>Something goes wrong. The GC blames the sub. The sub blames the drawings. The drawings blame the engineer. Nothing gets fixed. You are paying daily while everyone argues about who owns the problem.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-problem-card r">
        <h3>The Quality Surprise</h3>
        <p>The punch list is 80 items long. Drywall not level. Paint over unprimed surfaces. Millwork gaps. Now you are holding back payment while the crew makes three more trips to close callbacks that should never have existed.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-problem-card r">
        <h3>The Communication Void</h3>
        <p>You have no idea what is happening on site unless you physically go there. No weekly reports. No schedule updates. No heads-up on issues. You are managing your client's expectations with zero information.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

